<translate>
<!--T:11-->
This page provides an overview of '''copyright rules of Senegal''' relevant to uploading works into Wikimedia Commons.
Note that any work originating in {{wp-Senegal|Senegal}} must be in the public domain, or available under a free license, in both Senegal and the United States before it can be uploaded to Wikimedia Commons. If there is any doubt about the copyright status of a work from Senegal, refer to the relevant laws for clarification.

==Background== <!--T:12-->

<!--T:13-->
The French established rule of what is now Senegal in the 19th century. 
On 4 April 1959 Senegal and the French Sudan merged to form the Mali Federation, which became fully independent on 20 June 1960. 
The Federation broke up on 20 August 1960, when Senegal and the Republic of Mali each proclaimed independence.

<!--T:14-->
Senegal has been a member of the {{wp-Berne Convention|Berne Convention}} since 25 August 1962, the {{wp-Bangui Agreement|Bangui Agreement}} since 8 February 1982, the {{wp-World Trade Organization|World Trade Organization}} since 1 January 1995 and the {{wp-WIPO Copyright Treaty|WIPO Copyright Treaty}} since 18 April 2002.<ref name=Senegal-WIPO/>

<!--T:15-->
As of 2018 the {{wp-World Intellectual Property Organization|World Intellectual Property Organization}} (WIPO), an agency of the United Nations, listed ''Law No. 2008-09 of January 25, 2008, on Copyright and Related Rights'' as the main copyright law enacted by the legislature of Senegal.<ref name=Senegal-WIPO/>
WIPO holds the text of this law in their {{wp-WIPO Lex|WIPO Lex}} database.<ref name=Law2008/>

<!--T:16-->
The 2008 law repealed ''Law No. 73-52 of December 4, 1973 on Copyright Protection''; ''Law No. 86-05 of January 24, 1986'' and all earlier provisions contrary to the 2008 law.{{sref|2008-09 Article 162}} The former 1973 law, in turn, repealed the ''Law No. 57-298 of March 11, 1957, on Literary and Artistic Property'' (essentially, the [<tvar name="1">https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/loda/id/JORFTEXT000000315384</tvar> 1957 copyright law of France]).{{sref|1973 Article 14}} WIPO also holds the text of the 1973 law in their WIPO Lex database.</translate><ref name=Law1973/>

<translate>
<!--T:32-->
The 2008 law applied to works that had not yet fallen into the public domain.{{sref|2008-09 Article 161}}

<!--T:33-->
This did  not apply to posthumous works.{{sref|2008-09 Article 161}}

==General rules== <!--T:17-->

<!--T:18-->
According to the ''Law No. 2008-09 of January 25, 2008 on Copyright and Related Rights'', 
*The author's economic rights shall last for his or her lifetime and for 70 years after his death.{{sref|2008-09 Article 51}}
*The economic rights in a work of joint authorship shall last for the lifetime of the last surviving author and for 70 years after his death.{{sref|2008-09 Article 52}}
*The economic rights in a work published anonymously or under a pseudonym shall last for 70 years from publication or, if publication has not occurred within 70 years of creation of the work, for 70 years after such creation.{{sref|2008-09 Article 53}}
*The economic rights in a posthumous work shall last for 70 years from the disclosure of the work.{{sref|2008-09 Article 54}}
The periods provided for above expire at the end of the calendar year during which they would normally expire.{{sref|2008-09 Article 55}}

<!--T:34-->
The repealed ''Law No. 73-52 of December 4, 1973 on Copyright Protection'', as amended by ''Law No. 86-05 of January 24, 1986'', provided 50-year term of protection for most works and 25-year term of protection for photographs and applied art.{{sref|1973 Articles 40–41}} Prior that, the 1957 copyright law of France gave 50-year term for most works and 70-year term for musical works.{{sref|1957 Articles 21–23}}

